ial governor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-gong-of-cao"><span class="enc-name">Duke Gong of Cao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Gong of Cao ruled the small Zhou state of Cao in the 7th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-hu-of-chen"><span class="enc-name">Duke Hu of Chen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Hu of Chen, Gui Man, was the founder of the State of Chen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-qi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-huan-of-qi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke Huan of Qi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Huan of Qi ruled the state of Qi in eastern China from 685 to 643 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-zheng"><span class="enc-name">Duke Huan of Zheng</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Huan of Zheng was a younger brother of King Xuan of Zhou, who enfeoffed him with Zheng in the Wei valley in 806 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-hui-of-eastern-zhou"><span class="enc-name">Duke Hui of Eastern Zhou</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Hui of Eastern Zhou, named Gen, was the founder of the Duchy of Eastern Zhou.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-hui-of-jin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Hui of Jin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Hui of Jin, personal name Yiwu, ruled the Chinese state of Jin from 650 to 637 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-jing-of-qin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Jing of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Jing of Qin ruled the State of Qin from 576 to 537 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-mu-of-qin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-mu-of-qin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke Mu of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Mu of Qin ruled the State of Qin from 659 to 621 BCE and is often listed among the Five Hegemons of the Spring and Autumn period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-atholl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-of-atholl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke of Atholl</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke of Atholl is a title in the peerage of Scotland created in 1703 for John Murray, Marquess of Atholl.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-aveiro"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-of-aveiro.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke of Aveiro</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke of Aveiro was a Portuguese ducal title created in 1535 for João de Lencastre, a grandson of King John II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-of-saxony.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke of Saxony</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Duke of Saxony was the ruler of the duchy of Saxony in the Holy Roman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-shao"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-of-shao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke of Shao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">The Duke of Shao, personal name Ji Shi, was a senior member of the Zhou royal house who helped King Wu overthrow the Shang.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-of-zhou.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke of Zhou</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">The Duke of Zhou, Ji Dan, was a brother of King Wu, founder of the Zhou dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-town"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-town.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke Town</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Town, or Atakpa, is one of the Efik settlements on the Calabar river in south-eastern Nigeria, founded with Creek Town and Old Town in the 17th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-jin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-wen-of-jin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke Wen of Jin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Wen of Jin, born Chong'er, spent nineteen years in exile among other states before taking the throne of Jin in 636 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-qin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Wen of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Wen of Qin ruled the State of Qin from 765 to 716 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-teng"><span class="enc-name">Duke Wen of Teng</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Wen of Teng was ruler of the small State of Teng in south-western Shandong in the late 4th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-zhu"><span class="enc-name">Duke Wen of Zhu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Wen of Zhu was a ruler of the small state of Zhu, later known as Zou, in present-day Shandong.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-wu-of-qin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Wu of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Wu of Qin ruled the State of Qin from 697 to 678 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xian-of-jin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-xian-of-jin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xian of Jin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xian of Jin was ruler of the state of Jin in northern China during the Spring and Autumn period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xian-of-qin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xian of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xian of Qin ruled the State of Qin from 384 to 362 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiang-of-qin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xiang of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xiang of Qin ruled the Qin people from 777 to 766 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiang-of-song"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xiang of Song</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xiang of Song ruled the State of Song from 650 to 637 BCE and sought to become hegemon of the Zhou states.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiao-of-qin"><span class="enc-name">Duke Xiao of Qin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Xiao of Qin ruled the State of Qin from 361 to 338 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duke-zhuang-of-zheng"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duke-zhuang-of-zheng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke Zhuang of Zheng</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duke Zhuang of Zheng (reigned 743–701 BCE) was a ruler of the State of Zheng who made it the leading state of the early Spring and Autumn period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukes-chair"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dukes-chair.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duke's Chair</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Duke's Chair is a double stone throne on the Zollfeld plain north of Klagenfurt in Carinthia, Austria, probably assembled in the 9th century from reused Roman stones.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukes-laws"><span class="enc-name">Duke's Laws</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Duke's Laws were the legal code issued in 1665 for the territories of James, Duke of York, after the English capture of New Netherland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukhan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dukhan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dukhan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dukhan is a town on the west coast of Qatar and the centre of the country's main onshore oil field.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukla-pass"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dukla-pass.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dukla Pass</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dukla Pass is a low pass in the Carpathian Mountains on the border between Poland and Slovakia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dukuza"><span class="enc-name">Dukuza</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dukuza was the royal capital built by the Zulu king Shaka around 1827 near the coast north of Port Natal, in what is now KwaZulu-Natal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dulat-tribe"><span class="enc-name">Dulat (tribe)</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dulat are one of the largest tribes of the Senior Jüz of the Kazakhs, living mainly in the Zhetysu region of south-eastern Kazakhstan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dule-temple"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dule-temple.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dule Temple</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Dule Temple is a Buddhist temple in Ji County, Tianjin, China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duleek"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duleek.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duleek</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Duleek is a village in County Meath, Ireland, south-west of Drogheda.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/duleep-singh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/duleep-singh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Duleep Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Duleep Singh was the last maharaja of the Sikh Empire, installed as a child after the violent succession struggles that followed Ranjit Singh's death.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dulha-rai"><span class="enc-name">Dulha Rai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dulha Rai is the ruler traditionally credited with founding Kachhwaha power in the Dhundhar region of eastern Rajasthan, in the 11th or 12th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dulo-clan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dulo-clan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dulo clan</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dulo clan was the ruling house of the early Bulgars, recorded in the Nominalia of the Bulgarian Khans.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dumat-al-jandal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dumat-al-jandal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dumat al-Jandal</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dumat al-Jandal is an ancient oasis town in the Jawf region of northern Saudi Arabia, at the head of the Wadi Sirhan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dumbarton-rock.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dumbarton Rock</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dumbarton Rock is a volcanic plug on the north bank of the Clyde in western Scotland, known in Brittonic as Alt Clut, 'rock of the Clyde'.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dummer"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dummer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dümmer</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dümmer is a shallow lake in Lower Saxony, Germany, near Diepholz, the second-largest lake in the state.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dummers-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dummers-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dummer's War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1722–1725</span><span class="enc-snip">Dummer's War, also c