a ruined Cistercian abbey near Kirkcudbright in Galloway, south-west Scotland, founded in 1142 by Fergus of Galloway, perhaps with King David I, and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dundurn"><span class="enc-name">Dundurn</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dundurn is a hill fort near St Fillans at the eastern end of Loch Earn in Perthshire, Scotland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dungan-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dungan-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dungan people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dungans are Chinese-speaking Muslims; the name is used in Central Asia for the Hui people of north-western China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dungan-revolt"><span class="enc-name">Dungan Revolt</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt</span><span class="enc-snip">The Dungan Revolt was a major uprising of Muslim Hui and other peoples in north-western China in the 1860s and 1870s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dungannon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dungannon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dungannon</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dungannon is a town in County Tyrone, Northern Ireland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/dungarendra-singh"><span class="enc-name">Dungarendra Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dungarendra Singh was a Tomara ruler of Gwalior in the 15th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dungarth"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dungarth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dungarth</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Dungarth was a king of Cornwall in the 9th century, whose drowning in 875 is recorded in the Annales Cambriae.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunhua"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dunhua.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dunhua</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dunhua is a city in Yanbian Korean Autonomous Prefecture in Jilin province, north-east China, on the upper Mudan River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dunhuang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dunhuang</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dunhuang is an oasis city in the west of Gansu province, China, where the northern and southern Silk Road routes into the Tarim Basin divided.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dunkeld.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dunkeld</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dunkeld is a small town on the River Tay in Perthshire, Scotland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dunkirk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dunkirk</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dunkirk is a port city on the North Sea in the Nord department of France, close to the Belgian border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunnet-head"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dunnet-head.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dunnet Head</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dunnet Head is a peninsula in Caithness, Scotland, whose cliffs form the most northerly point of mainland Great Britain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunnottar-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dunnottar-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dunnottar Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dunnottar Castle is a ruined fortress on a rocky headland on the north-east coast of Scotland, south of Stonehaven in Aberdeenshire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dunollie"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/dunollie.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dunollie</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dunollie is a castle and early medieval fortress on a headland just north of Oban in Argyll, western Scotland.</span></a></li></ul>
